you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  alex sharma tells sky news the cop 26  summit  will go ahead in glasgow in november  a new initiatives launch to bring  together the net zero aims of big  financial institutions around the world  we speak to mark  carney who's chairing it and  replanting england's carbon-absorbing  seagrass  meadows  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions we aim to take you to the  heart of the climate crisis  explain the data driving the changes  that are already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come now the president of cop  26 has told sky news the uk government  is still planning for the summit to take  place in glasgow in november  it'll be the biggest climate meeting  since the 2015 paris accord  where countries agreed to limit global  warming to 2 degrees  ideally 1.5 degrees above pre-industrial  levels  alex sharma said plans are still in  place for the conference to take place  in person  although there will also be some virtual  elements  he spoke to our climate correspondent  lisa holland  i do not sense uh any desire from the  part of countries to delay  cop any further and we are working very  hard and planning on a physical event  taking place in glasgow in november and  actually what is really important is for  all countries the smaller countries the  larger countries  to sit together on a level playing field  at the same table  that is what i'm being told when i'm  speaking to governments when i'm going  around the world  and that is what we are working very  hard to make sure happens you can't rule  out surely that it won't take place in  some other form that it be slimmed down  that large parts of it will be virtual  you surely have to be planning for that  well as i said what what we want to  ensure and we're working very hard on  this is a physical cop but you raise a  very important point  about the virtual aspects of this and  i've been very clear right from the  start  is that i want this to be the most  inclusive uh summit uh  ever and of course uh we have learned of  the last year that you can do things  virtually  and if we can expand effectively the the  people are able to  attend uh virtually then we will do that  and that is precisely what we're looking  at now  but of course it's also really important  for these negotiations  that they happen in a physical manner as  i said that's what we're working on  planning towards  the uk has set itself this ambitious  target which has been applauded around  the world  to reduce emissions by 78 by uh 2035.  you'll be aware of some criticism that  the policies are not in place to deliver  on that  ambition what would you say to that  firstly look at the record of what we've  done  as i said since the year 2000 we have  been the country  of all the g20 countries the the biggest  economies of the world  where we have decarbonized the fastest  um we have set out uh some of the  policies so  uh the prime minister's ten-point plan  uh for a green industrial revolution uh  which i worked on  uh as well we set that out last year we  sent out the energy white paper  as well which set out a sort of a range  of policies and what we will be setting  out  before cop 26 is our net zero strategy  as well so people will be able to see  you know how we're going to make the  progress well ahead of the cop 26  meeting  40 world leaders have been invited by  president biden  to take part in a virtual summit on  earth day tomorrow  and there have been some significant  announcements ahead of that  the uk government says it will reduce  emissions by 78  by 2035 compared with 1990 levels  and today eu member states have reached  an agreement to make the block  climate neutral by 2050. well with me is  our diplomatic editor dominic waghorn  so dominic commitments being made ahead  of earth day so what else can we expect  from this summit  well first and foremost this is about  america it's about america restoring its  credibility on climate change after  donald trump  trashed it by pulling america out of the  paris climate change agreement  so it's about america showing it can  lead the way forward and restore the  confidence of its allies on this crucial  issue  so to that end we're expecting an  announcement about an ambitious goal  that the americans say they will  announce tomorrow no further details  more than that but they say it's going  to be ambitious  also significant is the russians and  chinese are coming as well so if you add  them to the indians  and the europeans and the americans it  means that the big the world's big  carbon emitters  all on board in terms of china we're  probably not going to hear  announcements from the chinese at what  is an american organized event  but there is a sense of progress between  china and america  in the light of the shanghai meeting  between the the chinese climate envoy  and his american counterpart john kerry  over the weekend  there's a sense that they agree on the  same goals but not necessarily  how to reach them it is vital that they  all do make progress at this summit  ahead of the cop 26 in november  because at the moment things aren't  moving in the right direction in paris  all countries agreed to  reduce emissions instead emissions are  going up and global  temperature average temperature is  increasing too domino thank you  well we've got an earth day special  episode of our climate cast  podcast that's out on friday and you can  find it  wherever you normally get your podcast  from now let's take a look at some of  the day's other climate news now and  a coroner's urging the government to  change the law after  air pollution led to the death of a  nine-year-old girl  ella kissy deborah died of an asthma  attack in 2013 after she was exposed to  toxic  air philip barlow's report says  ministers should set  legally binding targets to reduce  pollution pollution  levels ella's mom rosamund kissy deborah  says the government isn't doing enough  it's now or never and you know we're  going to cop 26  we are world leaders we need to show the  the rest of the world  that we take this seriously so it's not  about 20 30 20 40  it's about now the general public needs  to know  the impacts that toxic air is having on  their  whole being australia plans to invest an  extra  417 million dollars on projects that  will reduce  greenhouse gas emissions the prime  minister scott morrison  says the money will be spent on building  hydrogen producing  hubs and carbon capture technology mr  morrison has been widely criticized for  not doing enough to tackle climate  change  biofuel made from chip fat might not be  as environmentally friendly as first  thought a new study by the transport and  environment campaign group  suggests there's no way of proving used  cooking oil that's imported into the uk  and europe is sustainable according to  researchers  more than half of the used cooking oil  that's made into fuel in europe  comes from abroad  [Applause]  scientists are using robots to monitor  the world's oceans  500 floating monitors have been deployed  by the global ocean  biogeochemistry project to track the  health of the sea  the robots will measure various things  including the acidity and  temperature of the ocean  and people in britain are going greener  than ever before  a study by the wwf and university of  york has found the uk's  overall carbon footprint has fallen by  17  research showed a 25 rise in people  adopting a plant-based  diet and the number of people using  renewable energy has almost doubled in  the last  18 months un special envoy on climate  action mark carney is chairing a new  initiative to bring together the net  zero aims of many of the world's biggest  banks asset managers asset owners and  insurers  the former bank of england governor will  lead the glasgow financial alliance for  net zero which  acts as an umbrella organization for the  various net zero coalitions  launched by the financial sector well  earlier i spoke to mark carney and  started by asking him  about that scheme we're taking this  alliance of banks  and for cop for glasgow we're bringing  them together with pension funds  so-called asset managers  um and all the other parts of the system  that are committed to net zero  in one glasgow alliance well yes so that  private investment very important but in  terms of  of government spending are you  comfortable with the idea of the  government spending  a huge amount given that we're coming  out of a coronavirus pandemic which is  still  raging worldwide do we have the money  there to spend the issue is not  just what you spend but how you spend it  um and as we emerge and i'm  obviously not the one to call exactly  the point where we properly emerged from  uh  from the coronavirus crisis although the  signs are more encouraging in the uk  as we emerge it becomes more and more  important that the  weight of government the support from  government for the economy  is focused on the future is investment  as opposed to current spending  so the type of spending in the economy  of the future which is more sustainable  which  better integrates a digital economy that  becomes critical and u.s president joe  biden is hosting  the earth day climate summit this week  what would you like to see come out of  that  the first thing we wanted to see come  out of that is exactly what's being  announced today  um that the core of the global financial  system is uh ready and  focused and there for this transition to  net zero the second thing  is the type of commitments that uh the  uk government is announcing  uh greater ambition from the largest uh  industrialized countries  uh is very important obviously uh at  that  in that last regard uh what the united  states does um and the knock-on impact  of that um so uh we'll see higher  climate ambition  uh but as as you know anna this is in  the context of a critical year  for the climate and for the global  economy  because after this summit the uk will be  hosting the g7  in cornwall in june follow on through to  a series of events in the run-up to  glasgow with culminating in glasgow  uh in november seagrass is a climate  superhero it can absorb huge quantities  of carbon  clean surrounding seawater and stabilize  the seabed well today england's largest  sea grass planting effort got underway  off the coast of plymouth  to try to support and improve the  resilience of our marine  environment our science correspondent  thomas moore went along to lend a hand  off the coast of plymouth they're  planting hope inside these hessian bags  are seeds of seagrass  dropped to the bottom by volunteers from  the ocean conservation trust  they should grow into a lush underwater  meadow  seagrass is a bit of a super plant it's  really important  provides us many ecosystem services so  it traps carbon  from the atmosphere does this really  efficiently can be up to 35 times more  efficient than rainforest in  capturing carbon in its sediment  seagrass shelters five times  more fish than bare seabed yet 92  percent of the meadows that once  surrounded the coast have been lost  if britain's seagrass meadows were  restored to how they once were  they'd store up to eleven and a half  million tons of carbon that's roughly  three percent  of our annual emissions nature could  help us  the planet is heading towards 1.5  degrees of warming  and the tipping points that could lead  to the melting of vast ice sheets and  the die back of the amazon  but new research shows as long as it's  quickly cooled the worst effects of  climate change could be avoided  if we were to cross a tipping point  threshold then the message is  don't give up there might still be this  opportunity  that we can get back and avoid the  catastrophic change  nature removes carbon from the  atmosphere far more cheaply and  efficiently than  any existing technology replanting  seagrass is a start  and it could have a crucial future  thomas more sky news in plymouth  tomorrow on the daily climate show we've  got a bumper earth day program we'll see  how people around the world are marking  the day  and bringing all the key developments  from the leaders meeting hosted by  president biden that's at the same time  tomorrow on sky news  see you then

